- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked
Realistic sprint planning with bounded weekly windows; tests ability to form and optimize DP with small constant branching and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for fast range totals
- DP with fixed window length (2..7)
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time O(n) with a small constant, Space O(n)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minWeeklyCost(A, W) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(INF);
  const par = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i=2; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let k=2; k<=7; k++) {
      if (i-k < 0) break;
      const sum = pref[i] - pref[i-k];
      const cost = dp[i-k] + (sum - W)*(sum - W);
      if (cost < dp[i]) { dp[i] = cost; par[i] = i-k; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would follow using par[]
  return {cost: dp[n], prev: par[n]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative A values if allowed? 
- What changes if weeks must start on a specific weekday?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a grid-path DP with a bounded number of teleports, forcing state compression and a 2D prefix max to keep complexity at O(KRC). It tests correctness, backtracking, and memory considerations beyond standard path DP.

## Key Concepts
- DP on grid with at most k teleports
- 2D prefix max to propagate bestPrev for teleport transitions
- Reconstruction: store choice or re-run DP with pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxEnergy(grid, k) {
  const R = grid.length, C = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  let dpPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
  dpPrev[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const bestPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        bestPrev[r][c] = dpPrev[r][c];
        if (r > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r][c-1]);
      }
    }
    const dpCurr = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        if (r === 0 && c === 0) continue;
        let best = NEG;
        if (r > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r][c-1]);
        if (bestPrev[r][c] !== NEG) best = Math.max(best, bestPrev[r][c]);
        if (best !== NEG) dpCurr[r][c] = best + grid[r][c];
      }
    }
    dpPrev = dpCurr;
  }
  return dpPrev[R-1][C-1];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What is the time/space complexity and how would you optimize for very large grids?
- How would you implement robust path reconstruction for the teleport usages?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests dynamic programming on partitioning a string into numeric substrings, including numeric parsing and reconstruction, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on string partitions with exact k blocks
- Efficient substring value computation and leading-zero handling
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  // val[i][j] = value of s[i..j-1]
  const val = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (s[i] === '0') { val[i][i+1] = 0; continue; }
    let cur = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      cur = cur*10 + (s.charCodeAt(j) - 48);
      val[i][j+1] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = val[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        if (s[j] === '0' && i - j > 1) continue;
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] + val[j][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = j;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.push(0);
  cuts.reverse();
  return { sum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks instead of exactly k?
- How would you optimize for very large n or larger k (space/time optimizations, early pruning)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of circular constraints with DP, case splitting, and backtracking to reconstruct indices.

## Key Concepts
- Circular-to-linear reduction via casework
- DP with two indices: position and picks left
- Backtracking to reconstruct solution
- Time/space: O(nk) both, with careful base cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonAdjCircular(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  function solve(start, end){ /* linear range [start..end] */
    const len=end-start+1;
    const dp=Array.from({length:len+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
    const take=Array.from({length:len+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
    dp[0][0]=0;
    for(let i=1;i<=len;i++){
      const val=A[start+i-1];
      for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
        // skip
        if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t]; }
        // take
        if(t>0){
          const cand = (i>=2?dp[i-2][t-1]: (t-1===0?0:-Infinity)) + val;
          if(cand>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=cand; take[i][t]=true; }
        }
      }
    }
    // backtrack
    let i=len, t=k, idx=[];
    while(i>0 && t>=0){
      if(take[i][t]){ idx.push(start+i-1); i-=2; t-=1; }
      else{ i-=1; }
    }
    return {sum: dp[len][k], indices: idx.reverse()};
  }
  const r1 = solve(0, n-2); // exclude A[0]
  const r2 = solve(1, n-1); // include A[0] scenario actually requires excluding end points; this is illustrative
  return r1.sum >= r2.sum ? r1 : r2;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k selections or allow adjacency with a penalty?
- How would you extend to count the number of optimal selections as well as the max sum?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: exactly k non-overlapping subarrays maximize sum, a knapsack-like DP variant with efficient inner maximization. It mirrors budgeted promotion planning.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] is the best total for first i elements using t blocks
- Prefix sums P enable O(1) subarray sums
- Optimize inner max with best = max(dp[j-1][t-1]-P[j-1]) up to i
- Reconstruction via pointers to endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a,k){
  const n=a.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(let v of a) P.push(P[P.length-1]+v);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  dp[0].fill(0);
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best=-Infinity;
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      best=Math.max(best, dp[i-1][t-1]-P[i-1]);
      const cand=P[i]+best;
      if(cand>dp[i][t]) dp[i][t]=cand;
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k subarrays?
- How would you recover the exact endpoints from the DP table?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine subarray maxima with partition DP, requiring both maximization and reconstruction of multiple intervals. It exercises careful indexing and boundary handling.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a cap k
- Precomputation of maxSub for O(1) subarray maxima
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space tradeoffs and edge cases (k=0, equal values)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxSub = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, A[r]);
      maxSub[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let best = dp[i-1][t];
      let bestJ = -1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = maxSub[j-1][i-1] + dp[j-1][t-1];
        if (val > best) { best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best; take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const j = take[i][t];
    if (j === -1) break;
    res.push([j-1, i-1]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], subarrays: res};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on partitioning with a minimax objective, a practical budgeting scenario.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; - Recurrence: min over j of max of subproblem and block sum; - Reconstruction via parent pointers.

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch
function partitionMinMaxSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+a[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=pref[i+1];
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = Math.max(dp[j][t-1], pref[i+1]-pref[j+0]);
        if (val<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n-1][Math.min(k,n)], cuts: par};
}
```

